-- title:
--   Pullback comparison is an isomorphism for invertible ideal sheaves
-- statement:
--   Let $f \colon X' \to X$ be a morphism of schemes (in a fixed universe), and let $I$ be a quasi-coherent ideal sheaf datum on $X$. Assume $I$ is invertible in the sense of the project's predicate `IsInvertible`: every point $x \in X$ lies in a basic open $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}(s)$ for some affine open $U \subseteq X$ and some $s \in \Gamma(X, U)$, such that the ideal assigned by $I$ to the affine basic open $X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}(s)$ is generated by a single element $g$ of $\Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}(s))$ which is a non-zero-divisor. Assume also that the comap $I.\mathrm{comap}\, f$, the ideal sheaf datum on $X'$ induced by $f$, is invertible in the same sense. The conclusion is that the canonical comparison morphism $I.\mathrm{pullbackModuleComparison}\, f$ is an isomorphism of sheaves of $\mathcal O_{X'}$-modules. Here, for an ideal sheaf datum $J$, the module $J.\mathrm{module}$ is the kernel of the unit morphism from the structure sheaf to the pushforward along the associated closed subscheme inclusion, and $I.\mathrm{pullbackModuleComparison}\, f \colon (\mathrm{pullback}\, f).\mathrm{obj}\, I.\mathrm{module} \to (I.\mathrm{comap}\, f).\mathrm{module}$ is the morphism adjoint, under the pullback–pushforward adjunction for $f$ on sheaves of modules, to the morphism $I.\mathrm{moduleToPushforwardComapModule}\, f$ of sheaves on $X$.
--
--   This is the statement that for an effective Cartier-type ideal sheaf $I$ cutting out a closed subscheme $Z$, with invertible inverse image, the natural map $f^{*}\mathcal O_X(-Z) \to \mathcal O_{X'}(-f^{-1}Z)$ is an isomorphism; the invertibility hypothesis on the comap cannot be dropped. It is used in the theory of relative effective Cartier divisors on curves, to identify the pullback of $\mathcal O(-D)$ along a base change with $\mathcal O(-D')$ for the base-changed divisor.
